lines += ['### BFS comparison','', 'BFS finds minimum move counts of 3, 3, and 9 on the fixed 30-, 50-, and 100-square boards. Tabular Q-Learning and SARSA match the three-move optimum on the first two boards in final evaluation. On the 100-square board, neither tabular method won within the 400-step episode limit in final evaluation. BFS knows the complete fixed transition graph and solves a shortest-path objective directly; the RL policies learn from rewards and do not receive this graph-search solution.','', '### Interpretation','', 'The tabular methods reached 100% measured success on the 30- and 50-square boards across all three seeds, with three-move routes on both. Their checkpoint convergence appeared at episode 1,500 for those boards. The same tabular budget was insufficient for reliable 100-square performance.','', 'DQN and A2C performance varied considerably by seed and board; PPO solved the 30-square board across seeds but failed all three 50-square final evaluations at the given budget. Some DQN/A2C 50-square runs did succeed. None of the DRL 100-square runs solved in final evaluation. These outcomes show that increasing state-space size and longer trajectories can make learning harder under a fixed interaction budget.','', 'The board layouts matter in addition to their size: a ladder from an early square can create a short route, whereas snakes and overshoot penalties alter the value of actions. Q-Learning and SARSA had identical measured outcomes on the easier boards because both discovered the same short route; their update equations can nevertheless lead to different policies under persistent exploration or more difficult boards. The 100-square results distinguish them: seed-level cumulative rewards differ, but final success remained 0% for both.','', 'With fewer episodes, learning may still succeed on a board with a short, frequently encountered route, but performance is less reliable and seed-dependent. More training, more seeds, curriculum board layouts, or carefully tested reward shaping could improve results. Reward changes must be evaluated as a separate experiment because they alter the objective.','', '## 6. Answers to the assignment questions','']
answers=[
('Why can this game be modeled as an MDP?','The state is the current square, the action is the chosen die value, and fixed board rules determine the next square and reward from only the current state and action. The future is conditionally independent of earlier history once the current square is known.'),
('What is the difference between state space and action space?','The state space lists all positions 1 through N. The action space lists six choices, corresponding to dice values 1 through 6.'),
('How does reward affect the learned policy?','The +100 terminal reward encourages reaching the goal; the −1 move cost favors shorter play; ladder and snake rewards/penalties assign value to landing on those starts; −10 makes overshooting unattractive. Changing these values can change which route maximizes expected return.'),
('What is the role of epsilon-greedy exploration?','With probability epsilon, choose a random action to gather information about alternatives; otherwise choose the current highest-valued action. Decaying epsilon shifts behavior toward exploitation as learning proceeds.'),
('What is the difference between Q-Learning and SARSA?','Q-Learning is off-policy and bootstraps from the maximum next-state value. SARSA is on-policy and bootstraps from the next action actually selected by its policy.'),
('Why might Q-Learning converge to a different policy from SARSA?','Q-Learning targets a greedy policy even while exploring, while SARSA values the exploratory policy itself. With risky snakes, overshoots, and nonzero exploration, this can make SARSA favor a safer route. Their finite-sample estimates also differ.'),
('How do the number and location of snakes and ladders affect learning?','Their starts and destinations change transition structure, reachable paths, reward feedback, and the frequency of useful experiences. An early ladder can greatly shorten a route; a snake can undo progress. Counts alone do not determine difficulty.'),
('Can an agent learn a good policy with fewer training episodes?','Sometimes, especially when a short route is encountered early, but results become more dependent on initialization and seed. In these measured runs, tabular methods learned three-move routes on the 30- and 50-square boards within 3,500 episodes.'),
('What happens as board size increases?','There are more states and typically longer trajectories, so exploration and value propagation require more interactions. Original final policies and longer-budget policies at gamma 0.99 had 0% wins on the 100-square board. A separate gamma sweep at 0.80 and 0.90 produced 100% success across the tested Q-Learning and SARSA seeds. A 10,000-episode random-policy audit recorded 99.99% success, including one 400-step failure. These are results for the fixed layouts and budgets, not universal guarantees.'),
('How does the learned policy compare with BFS?','The tabular policies matched BFS’s 3-move routes for the 30- and 50-square boards. On the 100-square board, gamma-swept tabular policies reached the goal in 10 moves, one move above BFS’s 9-move optimum. BFS assumes the board is known and deterministic; RL learns from interaction and is motivated when transitions are stochastic, layouts change, or the model is incomplete.'),
('Why use RL when BFS can solve a deterministic board?','For a fully known deterministic board and a shortest-path objective, BFS is simpler and guarantees an optimal route. RL is useful when the transition model is unknown, stochastic, changing, or when optimizing a reward objective that is not identical to shortest path. RL costs more interactions and does not guarantee optimality in the finite budget.')]
for q,a in answers: lines += [f'### {q}','',a,'']
