lines += ['', 'The original 100-square random result was 100/100 wins. A larger audit recorded 9,999/10,000 wins; the single failure was episode 790 and reached the 400-step limit. The small original sample was therefore a close estimate, not a guarantee.','',
'**BFS audit:** an independent graph traversal reproduced all stored distances on the exact saved board layouts: 3, 3, and 9 moves.','',
'### Convergence audit','','The original CSV reports the first three consecutive evaluation checkpoints at or above 95%; it does not require the final policy to retain that rate. SARSA seeds 17, 29, and 41 on the 100-square board all first crossed at episode 1,500, but all three final policies had 0% success. For seed 17, validation remained at 100% through episode 2,500, then fell to 0% at episodes 3,000 and 3,500. This is a transient threshold crossing, not durable convergence. Audit rows distinguish first crossing from final sustained success.','',
'### Longer-budget experiment on the 100-square board','','The original CSVs and model files were preserved. On the same saved board and reward function, new seeds 71, 83, and 97 were run for 20,000 tabular episodes or 25,000 SB3 timesteps. Tabular learning rate/exploration settings and DRL hyperparameters were adjusted. All 15 final policies still had 0% wins and 400 mean moves at gamma 0.99.','', '| Algorithm | Mean final success | Mean moves | Mean cumulative reward | Budget |', '|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
for algo in ['q_learning','sarsa','DQN','PPO','A2C']:
    g=improved[improved.algorithm==algo]; budget='20,000 episodes' if algo in ('q_learning','sarsa') else '25,000 timesteps'
    lines.append(f'| {algo} | {mean_sd(g,"success_rate",100)}% | {mean_sd(g,"avg_moves")} | {mean_sd(g,"avg_reward")} | {budget} |')
lines += ['', '### Separate discount-factor sweep','','Per-step reward values stayed unchanged. With alpha 0.10, epsilon 1.0 decaying by 0.9998 to 0.02, 20,000 episodes, and seeds 71 and 97 per gamma, gamma 0.80 and 0.90 yielded 100% final success for both tabular algorithms. Each learned policy averaged 10 moves and cumulative reward 196. These routes are one move longer than BFS’s 9-move optimum.','', '| Algorithm | Gamma | Seeds | Success | Moves | Reward |', '|---|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|']
for (algo,gam),g in gamma.groupby(['algorithm','gamma']): lines.append(f'| {algo} | {gam:.2f} | 71, 97 | {mean_sd(g,"success_rate",100)}% | {mean_sd(g,"avg_moves")} | {mean_sd(g,"avg_reward")} |')
lines += ['', 'The same unchanged-reward tabular training at gamma 0.99 also produced 100%-winning validation checkpoints at earlier episodes. We replayed and saved the best checkpoints separately; four held-out sets of 500 rollouts each scored 100%, 10 moves, and return 196. Since the game and policies are deterministic, repeating a policy confirms its route rather than sampling independent transition outcomes.','',
'### Reward-loop diagnosis','','Greedy traces revealed repeat-state cycles. An improved SARSA trace accumulated +38 undiscounted reward across a six-transition ladder/snake cycle; a Q-Learning trace accumulated +14 over nine transitions. Original Q-Learning and SARSA also repeated an overshoot at square 95 for −10 per move. The +20 ladder reward can make cycling more valuable than reaching the goal under the discounted-return objective; the finite step limit does not add a failure penalty. Lowering gamma reduced distant cycle value in the tested runs. Cycle traces are saved in `results/audit/reward_loop_diagnosis.csv`.','',
'![Original versus improved success on the 100-square board](../results/improved/plots/original_vs_improved_success.png)','','Longer gamma 0.99 final runs did not improve; the separate lower-gamma tabular runs did.','', '![Original versus improved moves on the 100-square board](../results/improved/plots/original_vs_improved_moves.png)','','Best learned routes took 10 moves versus BFS 9; failures count at 400 moves.','', '![Discount sweep validation curves](../results/improved/plots/gamma_sweep_learning.png)','']
lines += ['## 8. Limitations and future work','', 'The improvement experiment tests one fixed 100-square layout and two seeds per gamma value. DQN, PPO, and A2C did not improve win success within the tested 25,000-timestep budget. Additional work could evaluate more independently generated layouts, increase DRL timesteps, or test stochastic/dynamic extensions. The exact original CSVs and policy files remain unchanged.','', '## 9. Conclusion','', 'The final audit confirmed all 45 original policy artifacts, reconciled evaluation metrics, and independently verified BFS. Longer training at gamma 0.99 did not improve final board 3 success. A separate unchanged-reward discount sweep produced Q-Learning and SARSA policies that win in 10 moves, close to BFS’s 9. The audit also exposed transient convergence and reward-loop behavior that the initial report did not fully explain.','']
